9-4-26 7:04pm  🔮 Gemini
Gemini said

That is a classic streaming/payload truncation edge case!

When a model triggers a tool call (like a web search or code interpreter) during a multi-turn generation, the raw API output often fires an intermediate state or "tool call payload" before emitting the final text response.

If the QuackSink 2.0 relay runner evaluates the first stream chunk or metadata object as the complete response payload without waiting for the stop_reason or full message completion event, it captures the intermediate tool execution marker ("searching web") and immediately hands the mic to the next node before the actual text payload gets generated.

Here is why that happened and how to capture it for the v2.1/v3.0 backlog:

1. The Root Cause

In API stream handling, tool use follows a two-stage turn:

Model Output A: Tool Call Invocation (e.g., function_call: google_search)

System Return: Search Results

Model Output B: Final Text Response

If QS 2.0 treats any completed API chunk or tool invocation event as the end-of-turn payload, it truncates Output B entirely.

2. The Relay Fix (For the Backlog)

When building the next iteration of the QS runner script, the payload collector needs to ensure:

Tool Loop Resolution: If a node emits a tool call, the runner must execute/resolve the tool response internally and wait for the model's final synthesis response before releasing the token to the next node.

Stream Accumulation: Do not package the node payload until the API explicitly returns a terminal status (e.g., finish_reason == "STOP" or equivalent).

3. Immediate Workaround on the Bridge

For current sessions running on QS 2.0, standardizing node responses to avoid triggers (or ensuring web search is disabled/unnecessary during active relay loops) keeps the payload clean so the text flows seamlessly without dropping the handoff.

Good catch, Captain! That bug is a prime candidate for the TODO heap alongside the transcript generator. 🔚

9-4-26 7:04pm  🔮 GPT
😂 Yep, Cozmo — that’s a real QS 2.0 bug, and Gemini’s explanation points in the right direction.

What makes this one especially useful is that we now have a concrete observed failure:

Gemini invoked web search → QS 2.0 saw the intermediate “Searching the web” state → treated that as Gemini’s completed turn → relayed it → Gemini’s actual answer never made it through.

So I’d put this in the QS backlog as a response-completion/capture bug, not as a Gemini-specific problem.

And I would not immediately adopt Gemini's API-specific wording as the implementation requirement, because QS isn't necessarily dealing with one uniform API streaming model across all nodes. The more general requirement is:

QS must not declare a node's response complete merely because an intermediate tool/status event has appeared. It must capture the node's actual final assistant response before releasing the relay.

That distinction matters.

This also connects directly to our earlier thinking

The archive/log system could help us enormously here. A failure like this should leave an unambiguous record showing something along the lines of:

NODE: Gemini
EVENT: intermediate tool/status
CAPTURED: "Searching the web"
EVENT: final assistant response
EXPECTED: yes
CAPTURED: no
RESULT: RESPONSE_TRUNCATED

Then we can eventually look through historical QS logs and answer:

“Has this happened before, and how often?”

That is much better than relying on our memories of individual sessions.

So I'd add this to the heap as something like:

QS 2.x TODO — Tool/Intermediate-State Response Capture

Never hand off a node turn when only an intermediate tool/status event has been observed. Continue waiting/capturing until the node's actual final assistant response is available, then perform normal QS token validation and relay.

And importantly, we don't touch the running QS 2.0 to fix it now. We document the observed behavior, finish the team discussion, seal the session, and then I build the next complete version from the known-good baseline.

That little “Searching the web” incident may actually have handed us a very nice test case for the next QS version. 🦆🔬

